#### 🧠 Core idea

> **You use cleanup in `useEffect` when you need to “undo” something your effect created**

#### ✅ 1. What is cleanup?

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  // setup
  return () => {
    // cleanup
  };
}, []);
```

👉 The function you `return` is the **cleanup function**

#### 🔄 2. When does cleanup run?

###### ✅ It runs in TWO cases:

##### ✅ A. When component unmounts

```text
Component removed → cleanup runs
```

##### ✅ B. BEFORE the effect runs again

```text
Dependency changes → cleanup runs → effect runs again
```

#### 📊 Timeline (important)

```text
Mount:
  → effect runs

Update (dependency changes):
  → cleanup runs (old effect)
  → effect runs again (new effect)

Unmount:
  → cleanup runs
```

#### ✅ 3. When SHOULD you use cleanup?

##### ✅ 1. Subscriptions (very important)

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const socket = connectWebSocket();

  return () => {
    socket.disconnect(); // cleanup
  };
}, []);
```

##### ✅ 2. Timers (setInterval / setTimeout)

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => {
    console.log("running");
  }, 1000);

  return () => {
    clearInterval(id);
  };
}, []);
```

##### ✅ 3. Event listeners

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  function handleResize() {
    console.log(window.innerWidth);
  }

  window.addEventListener("resize", handleResize);

  return () => {
    window.removeEventListener("resize", handleResize);
  };
}, []);
```

##### ✅ 4. Cancel API requests (important in real apps)

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();

  fetch("/api/data", { signal: controller.signal });

  return () => {
    controller.abort();
  };
}, []);
```

#### ❌ 4. When you DON’T need cleanup

##### ❌ Pure calculations

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  console.log("just logging");
});
```

👉 Nothing to clean up

##### ❌ Simple data fetching (sometimes)

If you don’t care about canceling:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  fetchData();
}, []);
```

👉 No cleanup needed (but aborting is better in real apps)

#### ⚠️ 5. Why cleanup is important

Without cleanup:

###### ❌ Memory leaks

* intervals keep running
* subscriptions stay active

###### ❌ Duplicate effects

Example:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  setInterval(() => {
    console.log("tick");
  }, 1000);
}, [count]); // ❌ no cleanup
```

👉 Every update adds another interval 😱

✅ Fix:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => {
    console.log("tick");
  }, 1000);

  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, [count]);
```

#### 🧠 6. Simple rule (remember this)

> If your effect **creates something persistent**, you must clean it up

#### 🔥 7. Mental model

Think of `useEffect` as:

```text
Setup something
→ React may replace it later
→ You must clean up the old version
```

#### ✅ 8. Quick checklist

Use cleanup when you:

* ✅ start a timer
* ✅ subscribe to something
* ✅ add event listeners
* ✅ open connections (WebSocket)
* ✅ start async operations you may cancel

#### ✅ One-line takeaway

> Cleanup in `useEffect` runs before re-running the effect and on unmount, and you should use it whenever your effect creates something that needs to be stopped, removed, or cleaned up.

#### 🧠 Scenario: Login Form (multiple states + actions)

Instead of juggling multiple `useState`:

```tsx
const [email, setEmail] = useState("");
const [password, setPassword] = useState("");
const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
const [error, setError] = useState(null);
```

👉 This gets messy fast.

#### ✅ useReducer version (clean + scalable)

##### Step 1: Define reducer

```tsx
type State = {
  email: string;
  password: string;
  loading: boolean;
  error: string | null;
};

type Action =
  | { type: "SET_EMAIL"; payload: string }
  | { type: "SET_PASSWORD"; payload: string }
  | { type: "LOGIN_START" }
  | { type: "LOGIN_SUCCESS" }
  | { type: "LOGIN_ERROR"; payload: string };

function reducer(state: State, action: Action): State {
  switch (action.type) {
    case "SET_EMAIL":
      return { ...state, email: action.payload };

    case "SET_PASSWORD":
      return { ...state, password: action.payload };

    case "LOGIN_START":
      return { ...state, loading: true, error: null };

    case "LOGIN_SUCCESS":
      return { ...state, loading: false };

    case "LOGIN_ERROR":
      return { ...state, loading: false, error: action.payload };

    default:
      return state;
  }
}
```

##### Step 2: Use it in component

```tsx
function LoginForm() {
  const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, {
    email: "",
    password: "",
    loading: false,
    error: null,
  });

  const handleSubmit = async () => {
    dispatch({ type: "LOGIN_START" });

    try {
      await fakeLogin(state.email, state.password);
      dispatch({ type: "LOGIN_SUCCESS" });
    } catch (err: any) {
      dispatch({ type: "LOGIN_ERROR", payload: err.message });
    }
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        value={state.email}
        onChange={(e) =>
          dispatch({ type: "SET_EMAIL", payload: e.target.value })
        }
      />

      <input
        value={state.password}
        onChange={(e) =>
          dispatch({ type: "SET_PASSWORD", payload: e.target.value })
        }
      />

      <button onClick={handleSubmit} disabled={state.loading}>
        {state.loading ? "Logging in..." : "Login"}
      </button>

      {state.error && <p>{state.error}</p>}
    </div>
  );
}
```

#### 🧠 Why this is better

###### ✅ 1. Centralized logic

* All state updates live in one place (the reducer)

###### ✅ 2. Predictable state changes

You can clearly see:

```
LOGIN_START → loading true
LOGIN_SUCCESS → loading false
LOGIN_ERROR → error set
```

###### ✅ 3. Scales better

If more fields/features are added:

* just add new actions
* no need to add tons of `useState`

#### 🔥 Another example: Todo App (classic but useful)

##### ✅ Reducer

```tsx
function reducer(state, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case "ADD_TODO":
      return [...state, { id: Date.now(), text: action.payload }];

    case "REMOVE_TODO":
      return state.filter(todo => todo.id !== action.payload);

    default:
      return state;
  }
}
```

##### ✅ Component

```tsx
function TodoApp() {
  const [todos, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, []);
  const [input, setInput] = useState("");

  return (
    <>
      <input value={input} onChange={(e) => setInput(e.target.value)} />

      <button onClick={() => {
        dispatch({ type: "ADD_TODO", payload: input });
        setInput("");
      }}>
        Add
      </button>

      {todos.map(todo => (
        <div key={todo.id}>
          {todo.text}
          <button onClick={() =>
            dispatch({ type: "REMOVE_TODO", payload: todo.id })
          }>
            Delete
          </button>
        </div>
      ))}
    </>
  );
}
```

#### 🧠 Key takeaway from examples

👉 `useReducer` shines when:

* multiple related fields
* multiple actions
* state changes follow clear rules

#### 🔥 Real-world pattern (VERY useful)

You’ll often see:

```tsx
dispatch({ type: "ACTION_NAME", payload: data });
```

👉 Similar to Redux — but local to your component

#### ⚠️ When NOT to use useReducer

Avoid it for simple state:

```tsx
const [count, setCount] = useState(0); // ✅ better
```

#### ✅ Mental model

> Instead of saying “set state directly”  
> You say “what happened” → reducer decides update

#### ✅ One-line takeaway

> `useReducer` is ideal for managing complex, related state by centralizing update logic into actions and a reducer function, making your components more predictable and scalable.

