#### 🎯 Key difference

| Hook              | When it runs |
| ----------------- | ------------ |
| `useEffect`       | after paint  |
| `useLayoutEffect` | before paint |

#### 🧠 3. Why this matters

Because you can:

* ✅ **read layout (size, position)**
* ✅ **modify DOM before user sees it**
* ✅ prevent visual flicker

#### ✅ 4. Your example (explained)

```jsx
useLayoutEffect(() => {
  console.log(ref.current.offsetHeight);
}, []);
```

👉 Why use layout effect?

* DOM is already updated ✅
* but not painted yet ✅

→ You get **accurate measurements**

#### ✅ 5. Real-world examples

##### ✅ A. Measuring DOM size (most common)

```tsx
import { useRef, useLayoutEffect, useState } from "react";

function Example() {
  const ref = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);
  const [height, setHeight] = useState(0);

  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    if (ref.current) {
      setHeight(ref.current.offsetHeight);
    }
  }, []);

  return (
    <>
      <div ref={ref}>Content</div>
      <p>Height: {height}</p>
    </>
  );
}
```

👉 Why not `useEffect`?

* User may briefly see incorrect height (flicker)

##### ✅ B. Prevent layout jump (very important)

```tsx
useLayoutEffect(() => {
  if (ref.current) {
    ref.current.style.transform = "translateX(100px)";
  }
}, []);
```

👉 Runs before paint:

* user never sees initial position (no flicker)

##### ✅ C. Synchronizing scroll position

```tsx
useLayoutEffect(() => {
  window.scrollTo(0, 0);
}, []);
```

👉 Ensures:

* page starts at correct scroll BEFORE paint

##### ✅ D. Tooltip positioning (real-world pattern)

```tsx
useLayoutEffect(() => {
  const rect = targetRef.current?.getBoundingClientRect();

  if (rect) {
    tooltipRef.current!.style.top = `${rect.bottom}px`;
  }
}, []);
```

👉 Needed because:

* positioning depends on layout
* must happen before render

#### ⚠️ 6. When NOT to use `useLayoutEffect`

##### ❌ Data fetching

```tsx
useLayoutEffect(() => {
  fetchData(); // ❌ BAD
}, []);
```

👉 Blocks painting → slower UI

##### ❌ Logging / analytics

```tsx
useLayoutEffect(() => {
  console.log("mounted"); // ❌ unnecessary
}, []);
```

##### ❌ Non-visual side effects

→ use `useEffect` instead

#### ⚠️ 7. Performance warning (important)

> ❗ `useLayoutEffect` BLOCKS the browser from painting

👉 If you overuse it:

* UI becomes slower
* worse performance

#### ✅ Rule of thumb

👉 Use `useEffect` by default  
👉 Use `useLayoutEffect` only when:

* you need layout info
* you must update DOM before paint
* avoiding flicker matters

#### 🧠 8. Mental model

```
Need to measure or fix layout BEFORE user sees it?
→ useLayoutEffect

Everything else?
→ useEffect
```

#### 📊 9. Timeline diagram

```
Render
  ↓
DOM updated
  ↓
useLayoutEffect runs ✅
  ↓
Browser paints (user sees UI)
  ↓
useEffect runs ✅
```

#### ✅ 10. Simple example comparison

##### With useEffect (may flicker)

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  setHeight(ref.current.offsetHeight);
}, []);
```

##### With useLayoutEffect (no flicker)

```tsx
useLayoutEffect(() => {
  setHeight(ref.current.offsetHeight);
}, []);
```

#### ✅ One-line takeaway

> `useLayoutEffect` runs before the browser paints, making it ideal for measuring or updating layout without causing visual flicker—but it should be used sparingly because it blocks rendering.

#### 🔥 For your level (important)

As someone building:

* large apps
* possibly microfrontends

👉 You’ll use `useLayoutEffect` mainly for:

* UI libraries
* positioning logic
* advanced interactions

Not everyday coding.

> **Measure first.** Most components do not need `useMemo`. Prefer it for expensive pure calculations or stable object/array identities passed to memoized children — not for `count + 1`.

#### 🔥 1. Expensive computation

##### ❌ Inefficient (recomputes every render)

```tsx
function App({ items }) {
  const total = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price, 0);

  return <div>Total: {total}</div>;
}
```

👉 Problem:

* Every re-render → recalculates total
* Even if `items` didn’t change

##### ✅ Efficient (useMemo)

```tsx
function App({ items }) {
  const total = useMemo(() => {
    return items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price, 0);
  }, [items]);

  return <div>Total: {total}</div>;
}
```

👉 Only recalculates when `items` changes ✅

#### 🔥 2. Prevent child re-renders (VERY important)

##### ❌ Inefficient

```tsx
function Parent({ count }) {
  const data = { count };

  return <Child data={data} />;
}
```

👉 Problem:

* `{ count }` = new object every render
* `Child` always re-renders ❌

##### ✅ Efficient

```tsx
function Parent({ count }) {
  const data = useMemo(() => ({ count }), [count]);

  return <Child data={data} />;
}
```

👉 Stable reference → prevents unnecessary re-render ✅

#### 🔥 3. Filtering large lists

##### ❌ Inefficient

```tsx
function UserList({ users, search }) {
  const filtered = users.filter(user =>
    user.name.includes(search)
  );

  return filtered.map(user => <div>{user.name}</div>);
}
```

👉 Problem:

* Filtering runs every render ❌

##### ✅ Efficient

```tsx
function UserList({ users, search }) {
  const filtered = useMemo(() => {
    return users.filter(user =>
      user.name.includes(search)
    );
  }, [users, search]);

  return filtered.map(user => <div>{user.name}</div>);
}
```

👉 Only recalculates when `users` or `search` changes ✅

#### 🔥 4. Sorting (very expensive in big datasets)

##### ❌ Inefficient

```tsx
const sorted = items.sort((a, b) => a.price - b.price);
```

👉 Problem:

* Runs every render
* Mutates original array ❌

##### ✅ Efficient

```tsx
const sorted = useMemo(() => {
  return [...items].sort((a, b) => a.price - b.price);
}, [items]);
```

👉

* No mutation ✅
* Cached computation ✅

#### 🔥 5. Dependency stability (useEffect bug fix)

##### ❌ Inefficient (causes infinite or repeated effects)

```tsx
const options = { page: 1 };

useEffect(() => {
  fetchData(options);
}, [options]);
```

👉 Problem:

* `options` is new every render ❌
* useEffect runs every time ❌

##### ✅ Efficient

```tsx
const options = useMemo(() => ({ page: 1 }), []);

useEffect(() => {
  fetchData(options);
}, [options]);
```

👉 Stable dependency → effect runs once ✅

#### 🔥 6. Styling objects (common hidden issue)

##### ❌ Inefficient

```tsx
<MyComponent style={{ color: isActive ? "blue" : "gray" }} />
```

👉 Problem:

* new object every render → child re-renders ❌

##### ✅ Efficient

```tsx
const style = useMemo(() => ({
  color: isActive ? "blue" : "gray"
}), [isActive]);

<MyComponent style={style} />
```

👉 Stable object → better performance ✅

#### 🔥 7. Derived values

##### ❌ Inefficient

```tsx
const completedCount = todos.filter(t => t.done).length;
```

👉 Problem:

* recalculated every render ❌

##### ✅ Efficient

```tsx
const completedCount = useMemo(() => {
  return todos.filter(t => t.done).length;
}, [todos]);
```

👉 Only recalculates when todos change ✅

#### 🔥 8. Large table / dashboard (real scenario)

##### ❌ Inefficient

```tsx
const processedData = processHugeDataset(data);
```

👉 Problem:

* heavy computation runs on every render
* freezes UI ❌

##### ✅ Efficient

```tsx
const processedData = useMemo(() => {
  return processHugeDataset(data);
}, [data]);
```

👉 Runs only when needed ✅

#### 🧠 Key pattern you should recognize

👉 Inefficient code usually looks like:

```
creating new objects / arrays / computations inside render
```

👉 Efficient code:

```
wrap expensive or unstable values with useMemo
```

#### ⚠️ Important: don’t overuse useMemo

##### ❌ Bad usage

```tsx
const value = useMemo(() => count + 1, [count]);
```

👉 useless — too cheap to matter

#### ✅ When it actually matters

Use `useMemo` when:

* ✅ expensive computation
* ✅ large lists
* ✅ object/array passed to children
* ✅ dependency for useEffect

#### 💡 Super simple rule

> If it’s expensive or causes re-renders → consider `useMemo`  
> If it’s cheap → don’t use it

#### ✅ One-line takeaway

> The inefficient way recreates values on every render, while `useMemo` avoids unnecessary recomputation and stabilizes references for better performance.

