#### 🧠 Quick reminder

> `useCallback` = **memoize (cache) a function reference**

```tsx
const fn = useCallback(() => {
  // logic
}, [deps]);
```

#### 🔥 1. Prevent unnecessary child re-renders (MOST IMPORTANT)

##### ❌ Inefficient

```tsx
function Parent() {
  const handleClick = () => {
    console.log("clicked");
  };

  return <Child onClick={handleClick} />;
}
```

👉 Problem:

* `handleClick` is recreated every render
* `Child` re-renders even if nothing changed ❌

##### ✅ Efficient

```tsx
function Parent() {
  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    console.log("clicked");
  }, []);

  return <Child onClick={handleClick} />;
}
```

👉 Stable function → child doesn’t re-render unnecessarily ✅

#### 🔥 2. Using with `React.memo`

##### ❌ Inefficient

```tsx
const Child = React.memo(({ onClick }) => {
  console.log("Child re-rendered");
  return <button onClick={onClick}>Click</button>;
});

function Parent() {
  const handleClick = () => console.log("click");

  return <Child onClick={handleClick} />;
}
```

👉 Problem:

* new function → props changed → `React.memo` useless ❌

##### ✅ Efficient

```tsx
function Parent() {
  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    console.log("click");
  }, []);

  return <Child onClick={handleClick} />;
}
```

👉 `React.memo` works properly ✅

#### 🔥 3. Passing callbacks in lists

##### ❌ Inefficient

```tsx
items.map(item => (
  <Item
    key={item.id}
    onSelect={() => handleSelect(item.id)}
  />
))
```

👉 Problem:

* new function created for EVERY item, every render ❌

##### ✅ Efficient

```tsx
const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => {
  console.log(id);
}, []);

items.map(item => (
  <Item
    key={item.id}
    onSelect={handleSelect}
  />
))
```

👉 Inside child:

```tsx
function Item({ onSelect, id }) {
  return <button onClick={() => onSelect(id)}>Select</button>;
}
```

✅ Now function is stable

#### 🔥 4. Dependency stability in hooks

##### ❌ Inefficient

```tsx
const fetchData = () => {
  console.log("fetching...");
};

useEffect(() => {
  fetchData();
}, [fetchData]); // ❌ runs every render
```

👉 Problem:

* `fetchData` changes every render
* effect keeps running ❌

##### ✅ Efficient

```tsx
const fetchData = useCallback(() => {
  console.log("fetching...");
}, []);

useEffect(() => {
  fetchData();
}, [fetchData]);
```

👉 Now effect runs only once ✅

#### 🔥 5. Inline handlers on plain elements

```tsx
<button onClick={() => setCount((prev) => prev + 1)}>
  Increment
</button>
```

👉 Fine for a normal button. Reach for `useCallback` when the function is passed to a **memoized child** or listed in a hook dependency array — not for every click handler.

#### 🔥 6. Expensive child components

##### ❌ Inefficient

```tsx
<HeavyComponent onAction={() => doSomething()} />
```

👉 `HeavyComponent` re-renders unnecessarily ❌

##### ✅ Efficient

```tsx
const onAction = useCallback(() => {
  doSomething();
}, []);

<HeavyComponent onAction={onAction} />
```

👉 Prevents unnecessary work ✅

#### 🧠 Key concept: reference equality

React compares:

```tsx
oldFn === newFn
```

###### Without useCallback:

```tsx
() => {} !== () => {}
```

###### With useCallback:

```tsx
sameFn === sameFn
```

✅ This is why it helps performance

#### ⚠️ When NOT to use useCallback

##### ❌ Simple cases

```tsx
<button onClick={() => console.log("hi")} />
```

👉 Fine — no need for useCallback

##### ❌ Overusing it

```tsx
const fn = useCallback(() => count + 1, [count]); // unnecessary
```

👉 Adds complexity with little benefit

#### 🔄 useCallback vs useMemo

| Hook          | What it returns |
| ------------- | --------------- |
| `useMemo`     | value           |
| `useCallback` | function        |

Equivalent:

```tsx
useCallback(fn, deps)
```

≈

```tsx
useMemo(() => fn, deps)
```

#### 🧠 Mental model

> `useCallback` = “keep this function reference stable”

#### ✅ When it REALLY matters

Use it when:

* ✅ passing functions to child components
* ✅ using `React.memo`
* ✅ function is a dependency of `useEffect`
* ✅ avoiding re-renders in large lists

#### ❗ Most important real-world rule

👉 `useCallback` is about **preventing unnecessary re-renders**, NOT just optimizing performance blindly

#### ✅ One-line takeaway

> `useCallback` keeps function references stable across renders, which is crucial when passing callbacks to optimized components or hooks that depend on them.

#### 🧠 Quick recap

```tsx
const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();
```

👉 What it does:

* Marks updates as **non-urgent**
* Keeps UI responsive while heavy work runs

#### 🔥 1. Heavy list filtering (most common use case)

##### ❌ Without `useTransition` (UI freezes)

```tsx
function App({ largeList }) {
  const [input, setInput] = useState("");
  const [filtered, setFiltered] = useState(largeList);

  const handleChange = (e) => {
    const value = e.target.value;
    setInput(value);

    // ❌ expensive filtering blocks typing
    const result = largeList.filter(item =>
      item.includes(value)
    );
    setFiltered(result);
  };

  return (
    <>
      <input value={input} onChange={handleChange} />
      {filtered.map(item => <div key={item}>{item}</div>)}
    </>
  );
}
```

👉 Problem:

* Typing becomes laggy
* UI freezes on every keystroke

##### ✅ With `useTransition` (smooth typing)

```tsx
function App({ largeList }) {
  const [input, setInput] = useState("");
  const [filtered, setFiltered] = useState(largeList);

  const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();

  const handleChange = (e) => {
    const value = e.target.value;
    setInput(value); // urgent ✅

    startTransition(() => {
      const result = largeList.filter(item =>
        item.includes(value)
      );
      setFiltered(result); // non-urgent ✅
    });
  };

  return (
    <>
      <input value={input} onChange={handleChange} />
      {isPending && <p>Updating...</p>}
      {filtered.map(item => <div key={item}>{item}</div>)}
    </>
  );
}
```

👉 Result:

* Typing stays instant ✅
* List updates in background ✅

#### 🔥 2. Search UI (real-world pattern)

##### ✅ Example: search suggestions

```tsx
function Search({ data }) {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState("");
  const [results, setResults] = useState([]);

  const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();

  const handleSearch = (e) => {
    const value = e.target.value;

    setQuery(value); // immediate UI update

    startTransition(() => {
      const filtered = data.filter(item =>
        item.toLowerCase().includes(value.toLowerCase())
      );
      setResults(filtered);
    });
  };

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={handleSearch} />
      {isPending && <span>Searching...</span>}

      {results.map(item => (
        <div key={item}>{item}</div>
      ))}
    </>
  );
}
```

#### 🔥 3. Switching tabs (UI responsiveness)

##### ❌ Without transition (laggy UI)

```tsx
const handleTabChange = (tab) => {
  setActiveTab(tab);
  loadHeavyData(tab); // blocks UI ❌
};
```

##### ✅ With `useTransition`

```tsx
const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();

const handleTabChange = (tab) => {
  setActiveTab(tab); // immediate

  startTransition(() => {
    loadHeavyData(tab); // non-urgent
  });
};
```

👉 Result:

* Tab switches instantly ✅
* content loads in background ✅

#### 🔥 4. Navigation / page transitions

```tsx
const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();

const handleNavigate = () => {
  startTransition(() => {
    setPage("dashboard");
  });
};

return (
  <>
    <button onClick={handleNavigate}>Go</button>
    {isPending && <p>Loading page...</p>}
  </>
);
```

👉 Useful for:

* large pages
* expensive components

#### 🔥 5. Updating charts / dashboards

##### ❌ Without transition

```tsx
setChartData(processLargeDataset(data)); // blocks UI
```

##### ✅ With transition

```tsx
startTransition(() => {
  setChartData(processLargeDataset(data));
});
```

👉 UI stays responsive while recalculating

#### 🔥 6. Form with expensive validation

```tsx
const handleChange = (e) => {
  const value = e.target.value;

  setInput(value);

  startTransition(() => {
    const errors = validateComplexForm(value);
    setErrors(errors);
  });
};
```

👉 Fast typing ✅  
👉 validation runs in background ✅

#### 🧠 Key concept: urgent vs non-urgent updates

| Type         | Example                             |
| ------------ | ----------------------------------- |
| ✅ Urgent     | input typing, button clicks         |
| ✅ Non-urgent | filtering, search, heavy UI updates |

👉 `useTransition` lets you say:

> “This update can wait”

#### ⚠️ When NOT to use it

##### ❌ Simple updates

```tsx
setCount(count + 1);
```

👉 No need — too fast

##### ❌ Critical UI updates

* form input value
* user interactions

👉 must stay instant

#### 🧠 Mental model

```
User interaction → urgent
Heavy UI work → transition
```

#### 🔄 useTransition vs useDeferredValue

| Hook               | Purpose                             |
| ------------------ | ----------------------------------- |
| `useTransition`    | control when updates are non-urgent |
| `useDeferredValue` | delay a value                       |

#### ✅ One-line takeaway

> `useTransition` lets you mark non-critical updates as low priority so React keeps your UI responsive while heavy work runs in the background.

👉 `useTransition` is especially useful for:

* search
* filtering
* large tables
* charts

#### 🧠 Quick recap

```tsx
const deferredValue = useDeferredValue(value);
```

> It returns a **“delayed version” of a value**, allowing React to **lag behind on expensive updates**

#### 🎯 Key idea

* `value` = updates immediately ✅
* `deferredValue` = updates later (low priority)

👉 Great for:

* search
* filtering
* large lists
* expensive UI

#### 🔥 1. Search input (MOST COMMON)

##### ❌ Without `useDeferredValue`

```tsx
function App({ list }) {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState("");

  const filtered = list.filter(item =>
    item.includes(query)
  );

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      {filtered.map(item => <div key={item}>{item}</div>)}
    </>
  );
}
```

👉 Problem:

* filtering runs on every keystroke
* typing becomes laggy

##### ✅ With `useDeferredValue`

```tsx
function App({ list }) {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState("");
  const deferredQuery = useDeferredValue(query);

  const filtered = useMemo(() => {
    return list.filter(item =>
      item.includes(deferredQuery)
    );
  }, [list, deferredQuery]);

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      {filtered.map(item => <div key={item}>{item}</div>)}
    </>
  );
}
```

✅ Result:

* typing is fast (uses `query`)
* filtering is slower (uses `deferredQuery`)

#### 🔥 2. Large list rendering

##### ✅ Example

```tsx
function List({ items }) {
  const deferredItems = useDeferredValue(items);

  return (
    <>
      {deferredItems.map(item => (
        <div key={item.id}>{item.name}</div>
      ))}
    </>
  );
}
```

👉 If `items` updates frequently:

* UI doesn’t freeze
* rendering happens in background

#### 🔥 3. Expensive computation

```tsx
function Dashboard({ data }) {
  const deferredData = useDeferredValue(data);

  const processed = useMemo(() => {
    return processLargeDataset(deferredData);
  }, [deferredData]);

  return <Chart data={processed} />;
}
```

✅ Keeps UI responsive when data updates rapidly

#### 🔥 4. Form input with heavy validation

```tsx
function Form() {
  const [value, setValue] = useState("");

  const deferredValue = useDeferredValue(value);

  const errors = useMemo(() => {
    return validateHeavy(deferredValue);
  }, [deferredValue]);

  return (
    <>
      <input value={value} onChange={e => setValue(e.target.value)} />
      <p>{errors}</p>
    </>
  );
}
```

👉 User types smoothly  
👉 validation is delayed

#### 🔥 5. Typing + live preview

```tsx
function Editor() {
  const [text, setText] = useState("");
  const deferredText = useDeferredValue(text);

  return (
    <>
      <textarea onChange={e => setText(e.target.value)} />
      <Preview content={deferredText} />
    </>
  );
}
```

👉 Use case:

* markdown editor
* code preview
* live formatting

#### 🔥 6. Combined with loading indicator

```tsx
const deferredQuery = useDeferredValue(query);
const isStale = query !== deferredQuery;

return (
  <>
    <input value={query} onChange={handleChange} />
    {isStale && <p>Updating...</p>}
  </>
);
```

👉 Lets you show:

* “UI is catching up”

#### 🧠 Important difference from `useTransition`

| Hook               | You control           |
| ------------------ | --------------------- |
| `useTransition`    | when to defer updates |
| `useDeferredValue` | which value to defer  |

#### 🔄 Mental model

```
Immediate value (fast UI)
        ↓
Deferred value (slow heavy work)
```

#### ⚠️ When NOT to use it

##### ❌ Simple UI

```tsx
const deferred = useDeferredValue(count);
```

👉 pointless

##### ❌ Critical logic

* authentication
* form submission

👉 must not be delayed

#### ✅ When to use `useDeferredValue`

* ✅ searching
* ✅ filtering
* ✅ large datasets
* ✅ live previews
* ✅ dashboards

#### 🔥 Simple rule

> If a value drives expensive UI → defer it

#### ✅ One-line takeaway

> `useDeferredValue` lets you delay updates to expensive UI by using a lagging version of a value, keeping the interface responsive during rapid changes.

👉 `useDeferredValue` is perfect for:

* large tables
* search bars
* analytics UIs

